        ":question/id": "773eaa1c-93aa-5388-970f-8375245372c9",
        ":question/problem": "If $c=\\begin{bmatrix}k \\\\ 3k \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},$ $d=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},$ and $c⊥d,$ then $k=${{field-1}}.",
        ":question/worked-solution": "We know that $c⊥d$ ($c$ and $d$ are perpendicular) if and only if $c\\cdot d=0.$\n\nComputing the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c\\cdot d & ={c}_{x}\\cdot {d}_{x}+{c}_{y}\\cdot {d}_{y}+{c}_{z}\\cdot {d}_{z} \\\\  & =k\\cdot (-3)+3k\\cdot 2+(-4)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-3k+6k-8 \\\\  & =3k-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c\\cdot d & =0 \\\\ 3k-8 & =0 \\\\ k & =\\frac{8}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
