        ":question/problem": "Let $g=\\begin{bmatrix}3{r}^{2} \\\\ -4 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}$ and $h=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2{r}^{2} \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$ Given that $g⊥h$ and $r>0$, find the value of $r.$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "We know that $g⊥h$ ($g$ and $h$ are perpendicular) if and only if $g\\cdot h=0.$\n\nComputing the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g\\cdot h & ={g}_{x}\\cdot {h}_{x}+{g}_{y}\\cdot {h}_{y}+{g}_{z}\\cdot {h}_{z} \\\\  & =3{r}^{2}\\cdot 4+(-4)\\cdot 2{r}^{2}+(-6)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =12{r}^{2}-8{r}^{2}-18 \\\\  & =4{r}^{2}-18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g\\cdot h & =0 \\\\ 4{r}^{2}-18 & =0 \\\\ 4{r}^{2} & =18 \\\\ {r}^{2} & =\\frac{9}{2} \\\\ r & =\\pm \\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe disregard the negative solution since we are told that $r>0.$ Therefore, $r=\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$"
      },
